  "full_response": "Attention mechanisms are a key innovation in **Transformer models**, enabling them to capture long-range dependencies and contextual relationships in data (e.g., text, images, or sequences). Here’s a breakdown of how they work:\n\n---\n\n### **1. Core Idea of Attention**\nAttention allows a model to **dynamically focus** on different parts of the input sequence when producing an output. Instead of treating all input tokens equally (like in RNNs/CNNs), it assigns **weights** to tokens based on their relevance to the current task.\n\n---\n\n### **2. Key Components of Attention**\n#### **(A) Scaled Dot-Product Attention**\nThe most common form of attention in Transformers is **scaled dot-product attention**, defined as:\n\n\\[\n\\text{Attention}(Q, K, V) = \\text{softmax}\\left(\\frac{QK^T}{\\sqrt{d_k}}\\right) V\n\\]\n\n- **Query (Q)**: Represents the current token’s \"question\" (what it’s looking for in other tokens).\n- **Key (K)**: Represents other tokens’ \"identities\" (what they can offer).\n- **Value (V)**: Represents the actual content of other tokens (what to aggregate).\n- **\\(d_k\\)**: Dimensionality of the key vectors (used for scaling to avoid large dot products).\n\n**Steps**:\n1. **Compute Similarity Scores**: \\(QK^T\\) calculates how much each query aligns with each key (dot product measures similarity).\n2. **Scale and Softmax**: Divide by \\(\\sqrt{d_k}\\) to prevent gradient vanishing, then apply softmax to get **attention weights** (probabilities summing to 1).\n3. **Weighted Sum**: Multiply weights by \\(V\\) to get the **contextualized output** for the query.\n\n#### **(B) Multi-Head Attention**\nInstead of computing attention once, Transformers use **multiple attention heads** in parallel:\n1. **Split** \\(Q\\), \\(K\\), \\(V\\) into \\(h\\) smaller matrices (heads).\n2. Compute **scaled dot-product attention** for each head independently.\n3. **Concatenate** the outputs and project them back to the original dimension.\n\n**Why?**\n- Captures diverse relationships (e.g., syntactic vs. semantic).\n- Enables parallelization.\n\n---\n\n### **3. Types of Attention in Transformers**\n#### **(A) Self-Attention (Encoder)**\n- **Input**: Same sequence (",
